-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d7e45505-307c-5593-ba89-29005b3e5902', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Listening to Night Migrants$q$, $q$Each spring and fall, millions of small songbirds cross the sky at night, too high and too dark to be seen. For a long time, the only way to count them was to wait for morning and tally the tired birds that landed in parks and woodlots. That method missed most of the travelers, because many birds flew straight past without stopping.

Researchers have found a better way to notice them: they listen. Many migrating birds give short, high calls while flying in the dark, possibly to keep in touch with nearby birds. Each species has its own call, often lasting less than a quarter of a second. A microphone pointed at the sky can record these calls all night, and trained listeners or computer programs can sort them by species.

In the fictional lakeside town of Harrowgate, a team led by ecologist Mireille Oduya placed twelve rooftop microphones across the region for three autumn seasons. The recordings captured more than forty thousand calls from at least thirty species. The team discovered that certain warblers passed over in large numbers on nights when almost none were seen the next morning. They also found that call counts rose sharply on nights after cold fronts, when north winds helped the birds along.

Listening has limits. A call does not reveal how many birds are flying silently, and rain or traffic noise can drown out the signal. Some species rarely call at all. For these reasons, Oduya's team compares its recordings with radar images and morning counts rather than relying on sound alone.

Still, the microphones have changed what the town knows about its skies. Harrowgate now dims the lights on its tallest buildings during the busiest migration nights, which the recordings help predict. A practice that began as a way to count birds has become a way to protect them.$q$, 'draft')
