-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f0f47798-5f61-5692-b34e-a0cf519c10fe', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Rule of Doing Less$q$, $q$When a museum receives a damaged tapestry, visitors might expect conservators to make it look new again. They might picture bright fresh thread replacing faded patches and torn sections rewoven so neatly that no one could tell. Yet most textile conservators today work by a very different rule: do as little as possible, and make sure anything you do can be undone.

This rule grew out of hard lessons. In the past, well-meaning restorers often repainted faded areas, glued backings onto fragile cloth or replaced original threads with new ones. Decades later, those glues yellowed and stiffened, the new threads pulled against the weaker old ones, and some repairs caused more damage than the original tears. Worse, the changes made it difficult to tell which parts of an object were original.

At the fictional Linden Hall Museum, conservator Paloma Reyes-Whitcombe recently treated a two-hundred-year-old wall hanging with large holes along its lower edge. Instead of filling the holes with new weaving, she stitched the weak areas onto a panel of dyed linen chosen to match the background color. The stitches hold the loose threads in place, and the linen shows through the holes without pretending to be part of the design. If a better method appears in the future, the stitches can be snipped and the hanging lifted away unharmed.

Visitors sometimes ask why the hanging still has gaps. Reyes-Whitcombe explains that a conservator's first duty is to the object, not to its appearance. A gap that is honest tells the truth about the hanging's long life, while a perfect-looking repair might hide it.

The rule of doing less can seem like doing nothing. In practice, it demands patience, careful research and great skill, because the goal is not to remake history but to keep it from falling apart.$q$, 'draft')
