-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2123051e-13ae-5606-908b-acb521fc62b7', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Finding the Way at Calder General$q$, $q$Anyone who has been lost in a large hospital knows the feeling: long hallways that all look alike, signs pointing in several directions, and a growing worry about missing an appointment. At the fictional Calder General Hospital, a survey found that nearly one in four visitors had asked a staff member for directions at least once during a single visit. Nurses and technicians were spending hours each week acting as guides instead of caring for patients.

The hospital asked a small design team led by Hamid Torres-Nakamura to study the problem. The team began by following visitors and watching where they hesitated. They learned that the old signs used medical terms many people did not know, such as "radiology," and that the building's three wings had been given confusing numbers over decades of expansion.

The team's changes were simple but connected. Each wing received a color and a plain name: the Garden Wing, the River Wing and the Hill Wing. Signs now used everyday words like "X-rays and scans" alongside the medical terms. Colored stripes on the floor led from the main entrance to each wing, and every elevator lobby displayed a map with a bold "you are here" marker. Appointment letters were rewritten to tell patients which entrance to use and which color to follow.

A year later, the hospital repeated its survey. The share of visitors who needed to ask for directions had dropped to about one in ten, and late arrivals for appointments fell as well. Staff members reported that they could spend more of their time on their real work.

The team points out that the hospital did not need a new building. It needed to see the old one through the eyes of a confused visitor, and then to change the information that visitors received at every step.$q$, 'draft')
