   $q$What is the central idea of the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Invasive ants are the greatest threat facing every wildflower in the Ashcombe Hills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Forest ants feed the fatty parts of seeds to their young inside the nest.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Mice and beetles eat so many seeds that wildflowers cannot survive in forests.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The hollowbell depends on native ants to carry its seeds to safe, rich soil, so protecting it means protecting that partnership.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The hollowbell depends on native ants to carry its seeds to safe, rich soil, so protecting it means protecting that partnership.$q$, $q$The passage explains the plant's problem, the ants' role, the benefits, the threat and the conclusion about protecting the relationship. Feeding the young is one step in the process, not the central idea.$q$, 'draft', '9bb116b0-d618-5546-8e82-4d075173c889')
