   $q$Which finding best supports the idea that the arrangement benefits the plant?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The hollowbell is a small wildflower that blooms in the spring.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Ants find the fatty attachments on hollowbell seeds irresistible.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Invasive ants eat the fatty tips on the spot and leave the seeds exposed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Seeds carried by ants were far more likely to sprout than seeds that fell beneath the parent.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Seeds carried by ants were far more likely to sprout than seeds that fell beneath the parent.$q$, $q$Higher sprouting rates are direct evidence that the plant gains from the ants' help. The ants' attraction to the attachment explains why ants take part, not how the plant benefits.$q$, 'draft', '9bb116b0-d618-5546-8e82-4d075173c889')
